\description{
Jointly estimates three marginal regression equations and a trivariate
Gaussian or Student-t copula by maximizing the full log-likelihood.
}
\details{
\strong{Margins.} Each margin has a linear predictor \verb{eta = X beta} and one
additional parameter estimated on the log scale: \code{gaussian}
(\code{y ~ N(eta, sigma^2)}), \code{lognormal} (\verb{log y ~ N(eta, sigma^2)}) and
\code{gamma} (mean \code{exp(eta)}, shape \code{exp(extra)}), \code{weibull} (scale
\code{exp(eta)}, shape \code{exp(extra)}; the mean is \code{scale * gamma(1 + 1/shape)}),
\code{invgauss} (mean \code{mu = exp(eta)}, \code{sigma = exp(extra)}, variance
\verb{mu^3 sigma^2}) and \code{beta} for responses in (0, 1) (mean \code{plogis(eta)},
precision \code{phi = exp(extra)}, i.e. \verb{Beta(mu phi, (1 - mu) phi)}),
\code{gpareto} (generalized Pareto for exceedances \code{y > 0} over a known
threshold that the user has subtracted: scale \code{sigma = exp(eta)}, shape
\code{xi = exp(extra) - 0.5 > -0.5}, \verb{F(y) = 1 - (1 + xi y / sigma)^(-1 / xi)}),
and the three-parameter \code{dagum} and \code{singhmaddala} (scale \code{b = exp(eta)},
\code{a = exp(extra)} and a third parameter with its own predictor, see
\code{shape}; all scores are analytic). For the
inverse Gaussian and Beta margins the derivatives of the CDF with respect
to the parameters use central differences. The additional parameter
may depend on covariates through \code{scale}, e.g.
\verb{log sigma_i = z_i' gamma}, which gives a location-scale regression in
each margin. Summary functions then report the sample-average scale or
shape.

\strong{Dependence.} The correlation matrix is parameterized by
\code{atanh(rho12)}, \code{atanh(rho13)} and \code{atanh(rho23|1)}, where \code{rho23|1} is
the partial correlation, so every real parameter vector yields a
positive-definite matrix. The Student-t degrees of freedom are
\code{2.05 + exp(.)}. Each of the three dependence parameters may depend on
covariates through \code{dependence}, e.g. \verb{atanh(rho12_i) = w_i' delta_12};
the correlation matrix is then observation-specific and remains positive
definite for every observation. In that case \code{fit$rho} holds the fitted
correlations per observation, and \code{fit$correlation}, \code{\link[=tc_dependence]{tc_dependence()}},
\code{\link[=tc_tau]{tc_tau()}} and \code{\link[=tc_taildep]{tc_taildep()}} refer to their sample averages. The degrees
of freedom are constant unless \code{df} gives a predictor for
\code{log(df - 2.05)}.

\strong{Pair-copula construction.} With \code{copula = "pcc"} the copula density is
\verb{c12(u1, u2) c13(u1, u3) c23|1(h12(u2 | u1), h13(u3 | u1))}, a simplified
C-vine, estimated jointly with the margins. With three Gaussian pairs it
equals the trivariate Gaussian copula in the parameterization above. The
derivatives of each pair copula's log-density and h-function with respect
to its two arguments and its parameter use central differences; they are
combined analytically by the chain rule. For a PCC fit, \code{fit$pair} and
\code{fit$ktau} hold the pair parameters and Kendall's tau per observation, and
\code{fit$correlation} is \code{NULL}.

\strong{Smooth terms.} Every formula (means, \code{scale}, \code{shape}, \code{dependence},
\code{df}) may contain penalized smooths \code{s(x)} in the notation of
\pkg{mgcv}, e.g. \code{y ~ z + s(x)} or \code{dependence = ~ s(x, k = 6)}. Bases and
penalties come from \code{\link[mgcv:smoothCon]{mgcv::smoothCon()}} with the identifiability
constraint absorbed. \code{s()}, \code{te()} and \code{ti()} are supported, with or
without a \code{by} variable, including smooths with several penalties
(\code{t2()} is not). The penalized log-likelihood
\verb{l - 1/2 sum_k lambda_k b_k' S_k b_k} is maximized, and the smoothing
parameters are chosen by the generalized Fellner-Schall iteration of
Wood and Fasiolo (2017) unless \code{sp} fixes them. For such fits \code{vcov()} is
the Bayesian covariance matrix \code{(H + S_lambda)^-1}, \code{fit$edf} holds the
effective degrees of freedom per coefficient, \code{logLik()}, \code{AIC()} and
\code{BIC()} use their sum, and \code{fit$logLik} is the unpenalized
log-likelihood. \code{\link[=tc_smooth]{tc_smooth()}} returns a fitted smooth with pointwise
intervals. The two-stage start is unpenalized. Unpenalized regression
splines such as \code{splines::ns(x, 4)} work in every formula without any of
this.

\strong{Estimation.} Unless \code{start} is supplied, starting values come from a
two-stage (IFM) fit: marginal ML for each equation, then copula ML given
the margins. The joint log-likelihood is then maximized by BFGS with an
analytic gradient (two scalar terms, the Gamma CDF shape derivative and
the Student-t degrees-of-freedom derivative, use central differences),
optionally from several starts, and refined by Newton steps. The observed
information is the numerical Jacobian of the analytic gradient.
}
\examples{
set.seed(1)
n <- 300
d <- data.frame(x = rnorm(n))
U <- tc_rcopula(n, matrix(c(1, .5, .3, .5, 1, .4, .3, .4, 1), 3))
d$y1 <- qnorm(U[, 1], 1 + 0.5 * d$x, 1)
d$y2 <- qlnorm(U[, 2], 0.2 + 0.3 * d$x, 0.5)
d$y3 <- qgamma(U[, 3], shape = 2.5, scale = exp(0.1 + 0.2 * d$x) / 2.5)
fit <- tricopula(list(y1 ~ x, y2 ~ x, y3 ~ x), d,
                 margins = c("gaussian", "lognormal", "gamma"))
summary(fit)
}
